{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-362696",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $108\\pi \\,{in}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=108\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =108\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{54}{r}-\\frac{r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}(\\frac{54}{r}-\\frac{r}{2}) \\\\ V(r) & =(54r-\\frac{{r}^{3}}{2})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(54-\\frac{3{r}^{2}}{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (54-\\frac{3{r}^{2}}{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 54 & =\\frac{3{r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(6)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nFinally, to find the largest volume ${V}_{\\text{max}},$ we substitute $r=6\\,\\text{in}$ into the equation for the volume $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{max}}=V(6) & =(54(6)-\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{3})\\pi  \\\\  & =(324-\\frac{216}{2})\\pi  \\\\  & =(324-108)\\pi  \\\\  & =216\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
